    "why": "THREE BODIES, AND THE FIRST ONE THAT CAN FALSIFY THE DEPTH LAW. Every co-tenancy result in this study is two-body. T18 was meant to close that and cannot run on this node -- and even if it did, dense3 is one decoder beside two pooling models, so 'the deepest tenant wins' is true by construction there. dense3b is T13's symmetric pair with a third body: two IDENTICAL Qwen2.5-1.5B instances plus one embedder. The two decoders start at equal in-flight depth, so the law makes a real prediction -- they should stay equal to each other while the shallow embedder is made worst-off. If instead MPS splits the two identical tenants apart, the two-body fairness result does not extend to three and every fairness claim in this study needs a tenant-count caveat. VRAM is comfortable: 3840 + 3840 + 2304 = 9984 of 12288, 81.2%. HOST RAM is the whole difficulty at 8600 MiB raw, which is why this tier ships with 35-swapfile.sh. Swap is an OOM BACKSTOP, not a resource: if the run touches it the latency numbers are void, so 36-three-body.sh records swap used before and after and says so."
